/*
Problem: Squares of a Sorted Array
Platform: LeetCode
Difficulty: Easy
Link: https://leetcode.com/problems/squares-of-a-sorted-array/

Approach:
The array is sorted, but after squaring, the order may change.

For example:
[-4, -1, 0, 3, 10]
Squares:
[16, 1, 0, 9, 100]

The largest square will always come from either:
- The leftmost negative number, or
- The rightmost positive number.

Use two pointers:
- a starts from the beginning.
- b starts from the end.

Compare the squares of nums[a] and nums[b].
Place the larger square at the last empty position of ans.

Then move the pointer whose square was selected.

We fill the answer array from right to left because the largest
squares must be placed first.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)

Important Tip:
When an array is sorted but squaring changes its order,
compare the absolute values of both ends.

Mistake I Made:
I initially thought I should fill the answer array from left
to right. But the largest square comes from either end of
the sorted array.

Therefore, I fill ans from the last index to the first index.

Another Important Point:
If the left square is larger, move a forward.
Otherwise, move b backward.

Pattern Recognition:
Sorted array + compare both ends = Two Pointers
*/
 
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> sortedSquares(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> ans(nums.size());

        int a = 0;
        int b = nums.size() - 1;

        for (int i = nums.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            int left = nums[a] * nums[a];
            int right = nums[b] * nums[b];

            if (right < left) {
                ans[i] = left;
                a++;
            }
            else {
                ans[i] = right;
                b--;
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
